  "abstract": "A private clique can fail to extend a coloring only when its exterior enforces a rigid boundary palette. We study a four-terminal relation in which two disjoint vertex pairs are rainbow and have equal color sets in every proper $k$-coloring. Our main theorem gives a uniform edge cost outside any induced vertex-critical $k$-core: if the remainder has $t\\ge4$ vertices and every nonterminal has degree at least $k$, it contributes at least $kt-\\binom{t}{2}$ edges. The same bound holds for $t=3$ under $K_k$-freeness. The proof classifies every possible degree-deficient remainder and constructs list extensions that contradict the forced relation. It requires neither planarity nor a minimum-order choice of core. A separate theorem shows that a $K_k$-free carrier with two vertices beyond its minimum core order has minimum degree at least $k$ and contributes at least $2k$ edges beyond a lower bound for its minimum cores. For biplanar graphs, these results and a small critical-core classification exclude nine-colorable carriers through order sixteen. The finite remainder is certified by compact positive coloring banks covering 475,180 attachment representatives, with independent standard-library checkers. The bounds also prove nine-colorability for biplanar graphs on at most twenty-two vertices containing the specified two-common-root private six-clique. We distinguish the hand arguments from the computational dependencies; no seventeen-vertex carrier or solution of the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem is claimed.",
